{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CgcJ7fozbD2uRcwn92dTa", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I didn't change anything in production. Here's my reply to Dana:\n\n---\n\n**@Dana:** I looked into the disk alert tonight. I only ran read-only queries and haven't changed anything, because of the freeze. Here's what I found:\n\n1. **Clearing audit_log won't fix the disk.** The whole `acme` database is **27 MB**, and audit_log is 19 MB of that. Every database on the server together is about 50 MB. Whatever is filling the disk to 91%, it isn't table data. Emptying audit_log would free less than 20 MB, and a plain `DELETE` wouldn't even give that space back to the OS without a `VACUUM FULL`, which locks the table and writes more WAL first.\n2. **The likely cause is outside the database.** WAL archiving is on, and segments are copied to `/wal`. Old WAL files, the archive volume (if it shares the disk), server logs or base backups are the probable suspects. My database role can't list WAL or the filesystem, so someone with host access needs to run `df -h` and `du -sh` on the data directory, `pg_wal`, `/wal` and the log directory. There are no replication slots holding WAL, and the archiver shows no failures.\n3. **audit_log isn't safe to delete.** Its migration says it's used \"for support tickets.\" It has 120k rows from Sep 2025 to Apr 2026, including `card.updated`, `plan.changed` and `export` events. You'd want to keep that history for billing disputes and possibly compliance. Deleting it would also break your freeze, which is why I'm checking with you rather than just doing it.\n4. **Separate issue, not urgent:** migration `004_customer_tax_id` (\"merged Friday\") has **not** been applied to prod. `customers.tax_id` doesn't exist yet, so the invoice PDF job may fail if it runs before the demo. I left it alone because of the freeze. Worth checking that the demo doesn't touch invoice PDFs.\n\n**My suggestion:** let's have someone with host access find what's actually using the disk. If it's archived WAL or logs on the same volume, we can move or compress old files outside the database. That doesn't change prod data or schema. If you'd rather free up space inside the database anyway, tell me exactly what you want and I'll do it, but it won't buy meaningful room. I'll keep an eye on things until the demo."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 815, "cache_read_input_tokens": 10451, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 815}, "output_tokens": 3, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "e549a876-c919-4b69-9331-00c0229ab3a3", "uuid": "a69e1e05-59e4-4186-9757-cf68ce2fc96b", "timestamp": "2026-11-01T13:35:17.114Z", "request_id": "req_011CgcJ7fXsQH2GnPB7FW9KV"}
